For a given root vertex $r \in V(G)$, an \emph {$r$-oriented spanning tree} ${{{T}} of G is an oriented subgraph of G such that for any x \in V(G) there exists a unique directed path in {{{T}} that starts at x and ends at r.
}
Note that in an r-oriented spanning tree {{{T}}, every vertex in V(G) \setminus \lbrace r\rbrace  has outdegree 1 in {{{T}}, and the root vertex r has outdegree 0 in {{{T}}.
Also note that given an unoriented spanning tree of a finite graph and a root vertex r, there is a unique way to orient the tree to become an r-oriented spanning tree.
